WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.680
Validation evaluates both the source and the specific information it provides.

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:08.065
Ask who created the material, when, with what access and expertise,

00:00:08.065 --> 00:00:09.762
and for what possible purpose.

00:00:09.762 --> 00:00:11.585
A credible publisher can make an error,

00:00:11.585 --> 00:00:14.915
while an unfamiliar source may provide a verifiable primary artifact.

00:00:14.915 --> 00:00:18.825
Distinguish primary sources, secondary analysis, and recycled reporting.

00:00:18.825 --> 00:00:21.540
Primary material is closest to the event or official record,

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.630
but it may still be incomplete or self-interested.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:26.740
Secondary sources can add expertise and context.

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:29.475
Recycled claims can create the illusion of broad agreement.

00:00:29.475 --> 00:00:32.425
Corroboration means independent evidence supports a claim.

00:00:32.425 --> 00:00:35.953
Ten articles repeating one anonymous statement are not ten confirmations.

00:00:35.953 --> 00:00:40.280
Trace assertions to their origin and seek sources with different access paths, methods,

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:41.235
or evidence types.

00:00:41.235 --> 00:00:45.435
Examine timeliness, bias, manipulation, and internal consistency.

00:00:45.435 --> 00:00:46.800
Infrastructure and roles change.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.564
Images can be genuine but miscaptioned.

00:00:48.564 --> 00:00:52.800
Old records can accurately describe a past state while misleading a current assessment.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:56.705
Look for credible contradictory evidence and document how conflicts were resolved.

00:00:56.705 --> 00:00:58.676
Use confidence language consistently.

00:00:58.676 --> 00:01:02.755
Confidence reflects source quality, corroboration, consistency, and remaining gaps.

00:01:02.755 --> 00:01:04.505
It is not the same as impact or urgency,

00:01:04.505 --> 00:01:07.795
and it should not be inflated because a conclusion feels plausible.

00:01:07.795 --> 00:01:10.415
When evidence remains unresolved, say so.

00:01:10.415 --> 00:01:13.560
Reporting an unverified claim with clear limitations is more useful than forcing

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:14.073
certainty.

00:01:14.073 --> 00:01:17.585
Record what additional evidence would raise or lower confidence and whether the

00:01:17.585 --> 00:01:20.720
requirement can be answered responsibly with available sources.
